    "body": "Helsing's The AD-1 Inferno and AD-2 Ambush interceptors. (Helsing)\nWASHINGTON — Helsing, Germany’s highest-profile defense tech startup, today announced a new air defense system and a pair of new interceptor missiles.\nDubbed Contra, the company is billing its design as an “AI-controlled autonomous air defence system designed to protect countries from scaled drone and missile attacks,” according to a press release . Helsing also claims the system can track and engage “hundreds or thousands of drone and missile threats at the same time.”\nThe company says Contra can integrate with existing air defense interceptors and counter-drone systems. However, the firm also rolled out two new, container-launched interceptor designs it hopes customers will purchase with Contra. The AD-1 Inferno is jet-powered, “capable of aerial loitering, swarming, and repeated re-engagements.” The AD-2 Ambush uses a solid-rocket motor and can reach Mach 2.5, with a 20 km (around 12.5 miles) range, per the release.\nThe company did not provide hard cost estimates for the two missiles, saying only that the price will “significantly [undercut] those of existing missile interceptors.” There is currently a major push across NATO nations for lower-cost interceptors, with Anduril , Lockheed Martin and CHAOS Industries all rolling out options in the last few weeks.\n“Our AI links existing sensors and effectors in real time and adds our interceptors, AD-1 and AD-2,” Helsing President and co-Founder Niklas Köhlersaid in the announcement. “With jet and rocket propulsion, a low-cost all-weather radar seeker, and two independent European supply chains, they deliver autonomous fire superiority up to ten kilometres high – affordable and at industrial scale.”\nBreaking Defense Video\nArmy’s tech chief talks autonomy, acquisition and the future of drones\nWatch Now »\nDrones take over AUSA, plus the Army’s plan to bring in new vendors\nWatch Now »\nHegseth talks autonomy at AUSA, a key show focus\nWatch Now »\nArmy doubles down on autonomy, stakes out Indo-Pacific role: AUSA Day 2\nWatch Now »\nTelle talks ‘manufacturing rodeo,’ supply chain ‘weak links’: AUSA Day 1\nWatch Now »\nNew leaders, new drone command: What we expect from AUSA 2026\nWatch Now »\nUnpacking Boeing’s F/A-XX win, plus a report from wartime Ukraine\nWatch Now »\nHow Hegseth envisions a new Autonomous Warfare Command\nWatch Now »\nWhat Europe’s military air fleets will look like in 30 years\nWatch Now »\nRiyadh gets closer to F-35s, Damascus presses sovereignty and an expert take on Iran\nWatch Now »\nScroll for more video",
